At the airport, the immigration officer
stretches his hand toward mine
as if asking to come on my journey,
and not for a bribe.

On my back, I carry the burden
of my mother’s prayers, and a backache
that will not leave me—
from all my journeys in yellow tin-coffin buses
probing the broken teeth and cavities
of Lagos roads 
like a dentist instrument.

At the border, the border-man
stretches his fingers toward my papers.

Excuse me, sir,
my body is terracotta.
My body is a journey
from another journey.
I am only a pilgrim, visiting
like the boy whose parents have no TV,
sitting in his neighbor’s room
for a World Cup match.

This green passport is not my body.
And a song is already a heavy thing
for only one mouth to carry.

I stand between two countries
like their translator.







 
